      "files": ["src/routes.js", "src/invoices.js"],
      "evidence": "In src/routes.js, getInvoice only authenticates the caller (line 2: `if (!req.user) return res.status(401).end();`) but performs no object-level authorization. It fetches an invoice by the user-supplied identifier `req.params.id` via invoices.findById (line 3) and returns the full object with res.json(invoice) (line 5) without ever comparing the invoice's ownerId to the authenticated user's identity. In src/invoices.js, records carry an ownerId field (e.g. { id: 'inv-1', ownerId: 'user-a', total: 150 }, { id: 'inv-2', ownerId: 'user-b', total: 220 }) and findById is a plain Map.get(id) with no caller/owner context. Data flow: req.params.id (attacker-controlled) -> invoices.findById -> invoice returned directly. Exploitability: any logged-in user (e.g. user-a) can request another user's invoice (e.g. GET /invoices/inv-2) and receive its financial data (total/ownerId); IDs follow a predictable sequential scheme (inv-1, inv-2, ...) enabling trivial enumeration. This is an IDOR/BOLA allowing cross-tenant disclosure of billing/invoice data.",
      "remediation": "Enforce object-level authorization before returning the invoice: after fetching it, verify that the authenticated principal owns the resource. For example, in src/routes.js after `const invoice = await invoices.findById(req.params.id);` add a check such as `if (!invoice || invoice.ownerId !== req.user.id) return res.status(404).end();` (return 404, not 403, to avoid leaking existence). Alternatively pass the caller identity into the data layer, e.g. `invoices.findByIdAndOwner(req.params.id, req.user.id)`, so authorization is enforced at the query/lookup level. Ensure every invoice retrieval path routes through this ownership check, and consider using unguessable identifiers for invoice resources."
    }
  ],
